      "source": [
        "### Addressing Class Imbalance\n",
        "\n",
        "As observed from the distribution plot, there is a significant class imbalance in our target variable `OPS_STATUS_CODE`. This can lead to a model that performs well on the majority class but poorly on minority classes, as indicated by the lower recall for classes 'P' and 'B'.\n",
        "\n",
        "One common technique to handle class imbalance in tree-based models like Random Forest is to use the `class_weight='balanced'` parameter. This automatically adjusts weights inversely proportional to class frequencies in the input data, effectively giving more importance to the minority classes during training.\n",
        "\n",
        "Let's apply this to our Random Forest Classifier."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "57147c9d"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Applying SMOTE for Class Imbalance\n",
        "\n",
        "To further address the class imbalance, we will use the Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique (SMOTE). SMOTE works by creating synthetic samples for the minority classes, rather than simply duplicating existing ones, which can help the model generalize better. We will apply SMOTE to the training data before training the Random Forest Classifier."
      ]
